[[:db/retract 17592186228327 :answer-field/choices 17592186228328] [:db/retract 17592186228327 :answer-field/choices 17592186228329] [:db/retract 17592186228327 :answer-field/choices 17592186228330] [:db/retract 17592186228327 :answer-field/choices 17592186228331] [:db/retract 17592186228327 :answer-field/choices 17592186228332] {:db/id 17592186229134 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f1b0e27c-532b-588c-9b00-39b1140a6bd7" "question-d7fb2c27-893c-5acc-87d5-bfee51ee7608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229135 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-585553db-a353-5891-ba13-8ab633961f14" "question-f1d39a1a-0d4c-51d0-9b27-2b99bcd83d50" "question-509105eb-35a0-5320-a66f-4c8711db0b98"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229136 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-82951619-49dc-5358-8bd0-2dda9a341d9b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228929 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1b0e27c-532b-588c-9b00-39b1140a6bd7" :question/id #uuid "f1b0e27c-532b-588c-9b00-39b1140a6bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337052" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5be79e0c9f09b5810459bfd8a1ce88f21e2e709dff9d20d7369681ddb61a81b.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6c40a60-2f1f-596b-9290-8a1e85cf9a1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6c40a60-2f1f-596b-9290-8a1e85cf9a1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c40a60-2f1f-596b-9290-8a1e85cf9a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd17c395-2231-51fc-bef5-8deee47ba9de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bde18c43-90a5-56cc-baeb-7e7d5b1dfb12" "answer-567936b2-10a9-5b49-8173-c727b9af89d9" "answer-fd17c395-2231-51fc-bef5-8deee47ba9de" "answer-c95372b8-4885-5240-92c0-f7e6f7aeb8f4" "answer-3a0306fc-e3d7-53a7-b259-ec89eda0dc93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bde18c43-90a5-56cc-baeb-7e7d5b1dfb12" :answer/id #uuid "bde18c43-90a5-56cc-baeb-7e7d5b1dfb12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-567936b2-10a9-5b49-8173-c727b9af89d9" :answer/id #uuid "567936b2-10a9-5b49-8173-c727b9af89d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd17c395-2231-51fc-bef5-8deee47ba9de" :answer/id #uuid "fd17c395-2231-51fc-bef5-8deee47ba9de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c95372b8-4885-5240-92c0-f7e6f7aeb8f4" :answer/id #uuid "c95372b8-4885-5240-92c0-f7e6f7aeb8f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{200}q=30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a0306fc-e3d7-53a7-b259-ec89eda0dc93" :answer/id #uuid "3a0306fc-e3d7-53a7-b259-ec89eda0dc93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d7fb2c27-893c-5acc-87d5-bfee51ee7608" :question/id #uuid "d7fb2c27-893c-5acc-87d5-bfee51ee7608" :question/math-academy-id "q-337107" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d928bacb5d981adffa5259c85c828e3be6610f0856085a18f7a090936e6f4d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.001\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.001}=1000q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+1000q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a5f3939-e1d3-5683-8305-3b6ffb5ac0cb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a5f3939-e1d3-5683-8305-3b6ffb5ac0cb" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5f3939-e1d3-5683-8305-3b6ffb5ac0cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-806dd943-de84-5557-b2b0-1acfe73db7da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f639a2f0-fbdc-5ee3-bea9-e417f2707610" "answer-d851ee1b-04df-5989-98cd-561927647a1c" "answer-0ab9a5ae-f057-5f84-88f3-7f8dc6599062" "answer-806dd943-de84-5557-b2b0-1acfe73db7da" "answer-9cbe0c8a-1b2a-521f-9bef-5b1df382057a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f639a2f0-fbdc-5ee3-bea9-e417f2707610" :answer/id #uuid "f639a2f0-fbdc-5ee3-bea9-e417f2707610" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+1000q=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d851ee1b-04df-5989-98cd-561927647a1c" :answer/id #uuid "d851ee1b-04df-5989-98cd-561927647a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+1000q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ab9a5ae-f057-5f84-88f3-7f8dc6599062" :answer/id #uuid "0ab9a5ae-f057-5f84-88f3-7f8dc6599062" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-806dd943-de84-5557-b2b0-1acfe73db7da" :answer/id #uuid "806dd943-de84-5557-b2b0-1acfe73db7da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cbe0c8a-1b2a-521f-9bef-5b1df382057a" :answer/id #uuid "9cbe0c8a-1b2a-521f-9bef-5b1df382057a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{10}q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229691 :question/problem "![](images/72/726e1c886236a47159bf154fc1ad6384ffc4aa89c5b3cba6b0067a70a0a773f2.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228327 :answer-field/correct "answer-bb52aa92-d16b-5b13-b73c-0ef4ab7409ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63c75ab7-18ac-5fd8-a7da-481e76a4f86c" "answer-bb52aa92-d16b-5b13-b73c-0ef4ab7409ba" "answer-8141572d-0cb5-5437-81ef-db44d556371d" "answer-45cbf82e-ad41-5140-8fae-7e78b5c82e4d" "answer-55cf4be4-f777-507f-9855-dc4c10df153f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63c75ab7-18ac-5fd8-a7da-481e76a4f86c" :answer/id #uuid "63c75ab7-18ac-5fd8-a7da-481e76a4f86c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb52aa92-d16b-5b13-b73c-0ef4ab7409ba" :answer/id #uuid "bb52aa92-d16b-5b13-b73c-0ef4ab7409ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8141572d-0cb5-5437-81ef-db44d556371d" :answer/id #uuid "8141572d-0cb5-5437-81ef-db44d556371d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45cbf82e-ad41-5140-8fae-7e78b5c82e4d" :answer/id #uuid "45cbf82e-ad41-5140-8fae-7e78b5c82e4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55cf4be4-f777-507f-9855-dc4c10df153f" :answer/id #uuid "55cf4be4-f777-507f-9855-dc4c10df153f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{10}q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-585553db-a353-5891-ba13-8ab633961f14" :question/id #uuid "585553db-a353-5891-ba13-8ab633961f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-337118" :question/problem "![](images/69/693b38a5c1e5e07fc2791b3d7a471eae47382326a771c1e6ebc49d3cbd167c16.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{9}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{20},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/9}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{250}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(27-2{e}^{-50t/9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{27}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6401de53-6a6f-5a0d-853a-49b1c6118136"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6401de53-6a6f-5a0d-853a-49b1c6118136" :answer-field/id #uuid "6401de53-6a6f-5a0d-853a-49b1c6118136" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-30dde241-c39f-565a-b89e-a8fa62b979c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-30dde241-c39f-565a-b89e-a8fa62b979c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30dde241-c39f-565a-b89e-a8fa62b979c5" :answer/id #uuid "30dde241-c39f-565a-b89e-a8fa62b979c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{500}(27-2e^{-50t/9})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1d39a1a-0d4c-51d0-9b27-2b99bcd83d50" :question/id #uuid "f1d39a1a-0d4c-51d0-9b27-2b99bcd83d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-337110" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/worked-solution "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =\\frac{3}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{32}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{3}{32}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{32}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{32}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aea3f0d9-d10a-5139-ac86-db3f828cbad9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aea3f0d9-d10a-5139-ac86-db3f828cbad9" :answer-field/id #uuid "aea3f0d9-d10a-5139-ac86-db3f828cbad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4cf57df7-2157-5ced-941e-1fca4628c873" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cf57df7-2157-5ced-941e-1fca4628c873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cf57df7-2157-5ced-941e-1fca4628c873" :answer/id #uuid "4cf57df7-2157-5ced-941e-1fca4628c873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-509105eb-35a0-5320-a66f-4c8711db0b98" :question/id #uuid "509105eb-35a0-5320-a66f-4c8711db0b98" :question/math-academy-id "q-337117" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e22c8502d1949e3698da9ebd630d746c74be0c8d590e5bbfa1096d9a3cf5e1dc.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{4}{25}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{2}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{100},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{2}{125} & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{3}{500}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{2}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(8-3{e}^{-10t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{3}{500}{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da3b531d-5cea-59a4-b095-e5fc0e7a06c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da3b531d-5cea-59a4-b095-e5fc0e7a06c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "da3b531d-5cea-59a4-b095-e5fc0e7a06c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-775c4fbe-adfa-5c6e-9980-830b523b75a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-775c4fbe-adfa-5c6e-9980-830b523b75a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-775c4fbe-adfa-5c6e-9980-830b523b75a6" :answer/id #uuid "775c4fbe-adfa-5c6e-9980-830b523b75a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{500}(8-3e^{-10t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82951619-49dc-5358-8bd0-2dda9a341d9b" :question/id #uuid "82951619-49dc-5358-8bd0-2dda9a341d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337139" :question/problem "![](images/94/94befb5500eff8908ded191ea1bd018347718823c34f9fbf369791d37708c72c.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{Q}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $200\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=200\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=200I=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{1}{25}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{425},\\beta =\\frac{1}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-4,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-4+1=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d53980ff-5f51-516b-b4df-e3f2a87df48f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d53980ff-5f51-516b-b4df-e3f2a87df48f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d53980ff-5f51-516b-b4df-e3f2a87df48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a668aa65-d8e6-5d35-b32e-66a27f6ff3eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7f57542-e016-5e02-8b58-733f37ed44ca" "answer-a668aa65-d8e6-5d35-b32e-66a27f6ff3eb" "answer-a9a1e65e-7b75-5213-994e-a3123784ed19" "answer-f015ad91-9c18-5c41-82b2-d47d72dffa77" "answer-33f25045-3cdd-5e38-a797-7e5f3da09fe1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f57542-e016-5e02-8b58-733f37ed44ca" :answer/id #uuid "c7f57542-e016-5e02-8b58-733f37ed44ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a668aa65-d8e6-5d35-b32e-66a27f6ff3eb" :answer/id #uuid "a668aa65-d8e6-5d35-b32e-66a27f6ff3eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9a1e65e-7b75-5213-994e-a3123784ed19" :answer/id #uuid "a9a1e65e-7b75-5213-994e-a3123784ed19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f015ad91-9c18-5c41-82b2-d47d72dffa77" :answer/id #uuid "f015ad91-9c18-5c41-82b2-d47d72dffa77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33f25045-3cdd-5e38-a797-7e5f3da09fe1" :answer/id #uuid "33f25045-3cdd-5e38-a797-7e5f3da09fe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311846 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{16}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{20}{41})}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311842 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{10}{41},\\beta =\\frac{8}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{41})}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229702 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4182416a97e5f0111b0823d6699f0ed39125f60289d7c7ebbb5f98d51d1a0f6.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{60})}=60q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+60q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+3{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{25},\\beta =\\frac{4}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{25})}^{2}}=0.2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228391 :answer-field/correct 17592186228393 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311843 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230207 :tutorial/content "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta =\\frac{2}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229262 :activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186171069 :answer-field/choices 17592186171072] [:db/retract 17592186171069 :answer-field/choices 17592186171073] [:db/retract 17592186171069 :answer-field/choices 17592186171074] [:db/retract 17592186171069 :answer-field/choices 17592186171071] {:db/id 17592186172015 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-176b69af-2301-5857-a64b-7b0669d9dca3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171819 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|{-3z}|-5\\cdot|4z|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|3z|-5\\cdot|4z|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3z|-5\\cdot |4z| & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |z|-5\\cdot |4|\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |z|-5\\cdot 4\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |z|-20\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3|z|-20|z| & = \\\\ -17|z| & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172111 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3661ca5-897d-5020-9895-05a1bf678821" "question-7d1d6d3a-1caf-53a1-9cd7-47e7cd04105c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171714 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{|10|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x^2 \\geq 0$ for any real $x,$ we have that $\\left|x^2\\right|=x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{10} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172112 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13de8b24-aaa0-5ec2-ad2b-e4d7d0eec094" "question-e5e4373f-fc17-548d-be9f-bc3f6efcbaf7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171715 :question/problem "Given that $x\\lt 0,$ simplify $|{-6x}|+2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|6x|+2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6x|+2x & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6|x|+2x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x \\lt 0,$ we have that $|x|=-x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6|x|+2x & = \\\\ 6(-x)+2x & = \\\\ -6x+2x & = \\\\ -4x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-176b69af-2301-5857-a64b-7b0669d9dca3" :question/id #uuid "176b69af-2301-5857-a64b-7b0669d9dca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-52130" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $2\\cdot |-2s|+3.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n2\\cdot |2s|+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |2s|+3 & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |2|\\cdot |s|+3 & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 2\\cdot |s|+3 & = \\\\ 4|s|+3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5c1e571-065f-56e1-b547-c5d8576a10bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5c1e571-065f-56e1-b547-c5d8576a10bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c1e571-065f-56e1-b547-c5d8576a10bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aa13e59a-2a4d-535a-9aff-88818af6a236" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccc825dd-39a6-5b11-a839-62ca6e000973" "answer-6da3c059-e7a2-561d-b24f-ae1a025126bc" "answer-db6d064b-135a-5826-aa11-e1ee2c639e09" "answer-ae900989-fa31-5ec7-a8af-afd82d4149e6" "answer-aa13e59a-2a4d-535a-9aff-88818af6a236"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccc825dd-39a6-5b11-a839-62ca6e000973" :answer/id #uuid "ccc825dd-39a6-5b11-a839-62ca6e000973" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4|s|+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6da3c059-e7a2-561d-b24f-ae1a025126bc" :answer/id #uuid "6da3c059-e7a2-561d-b24f-ae1a025126bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|s|+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db6d064b-135a-5826-aa11-e1ee2c639e09" :answer/id #uuid "db6d064b-135a-5826-aa11-e1ee2c639e09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4s+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae900989-fa31-5ec7-a8af-afd82d4149e6" :answer/id #uuid "ae900989-fa31-5ec7-a8af-afd82d4149e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4s-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa13e59a-2a4d-535a-9aff-88818af6a236" :answer/id #uuid "aa13e59a-2a4d-535a-9aff-88818af6a236" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4|s|+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172574 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-5x|-2\\cdot |-2x|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|-2\\cdot |2x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|-2\\cdot |2x| & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |x|-2\\cdot |2|\\cdot |x| & = \\\\ 5|x|-2\\cdot 2\\cdot |x| & = \\\\ 5|x|-4|x| & = \\\\ |x| & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171069 :answer-field/correct "answer-10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16063982-ad9c-5993-87f3-955d9e76798a" "answer-6bcc98a2-495e-5717-bd28-655a11483875" "answer-c2fe28bf-bb1b-528d-8fe1-bb8dad80372e" "answer-10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16063982-ad9c-5993-87f3-955d9e76798a" :answer/id #uuid "16063982-ad9c-5993-87f3-955d9e76798a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bcc98a2-495e-5717-bd28-655a11483875" :answer/id #uuid "6bcc98a2-495e-5717-bd28-655a11483875" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2fe28bf-bb1b-528d-8fe1-bb8dad80372e" :answer/id #uuid "c2fe28bf-bb1b-528d-8fe1-bb8dad80372e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d" :answer/id #uuid "10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3661ca5-897d-5020-9895-05a1bf678821" :question/id #uuid "e3661ca5-897d-5020-9895-05a1bf678821" :question/math-academy-id "q-112119" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-|3{x}^{2}|-20{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for any real $x,$ we have that $|{x}^{2}|={x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3|{x}^{2}|-20{x}^{2} & = \\\\ -3{x}^{2}-20{x}^{2} & = \\\\ -23{x}^{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e64f76b-2425-5767-b9ca-e2c7784c239d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e64f76b-2425-5767-b9ca-e2c7784c239d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e64f76b-2425-5767-b9ca-e2c7784c239d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-321ca6e7-d285-51c9-adf9-dfbc55c99850" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a52788c1-3a77-5c4b-ade7-61069bf6f83e" "answer-6213ccd5-c307-524b-bc39-8b8b7e2483b6" "answer-321ca6e7-d285-51c9-adf9-dfbc55c99850" "answer-5d0bcf01-4cbe-5eb8-9d07-d7f0a66f2376" "answer-21bf1704-ae50-5b54-a182-325089ab00d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a52788c1-3a77-5c4b-ade7-61069bf6f83e" :answer/id #uuid "a52788c1-3a77-5c4b-ade7-61069bf6f83e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-17{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6213ccd5-c307-524b-bc39-8b8b7e2483b6" :answer/id #uuid "6213ccd5-c307-524b-bc39-8b8b7e2483b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-20{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-321ca6e7-d285-51c9-adf9-dfbc55c99850" :answer/id #uuid "321ca6e7-d285-51c9-adf9-dfbc55c99850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-23{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d0bcf01-4cbe-5eb8-9d07-d7f0a66f2376" :answer/id #uuid "5d0bcf01-4cbe-5eb8-9d07-d7f0a66f2376" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21bf1704-ae50-5b54-a182-325089ab00d9" :answer/id #uuid "21bf1704-ae50-5b54-a182-325089ab00d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d1d6d3a-1caf-53a1-9cd7-47e7cd04105c" :question/id #uuid "7d1d6d3a-1caf-53a1-9cd7-47e7cd04105c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78941" :question/problem "$|-\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|2{k}^{2}|}{|3|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |{k}^{2}|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2|{k}^{2}|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${k}^{2}\\ge 0$ for any real $k,$ we have that $|{k}^{2}|={k}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2|{k}^{2}|}{3} & =\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbfbd916-b1d3-545a-8514-bc1cb5dfc2a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbfbd916-b1d3-545a-8514-bc1cb5dfc2a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbfbd916-b1d3-545a-8514-bc1cb5dfc2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8824de24-d3dc-5d98-b0ca-f43ee7699f9e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22d42365-3034-57f9-b80d-03413fa89807" "answer-4cfef805-d8bd-5a29-b7b3-ef894a92d11c" "answer-bf56fbd2-f5c2-579c-8ebb-6d499c95b621" "answer-0fc0bc59-3785-5eda-b8fc-f947d9ab58ca" "answer-8824de24-d3dc-5d98-b0ca-f43ee7699f9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d42365-3034-57f9-b80d-03413fa89807" :answer/id #uuid "22d42365-3034-57f9-b80d-03413fa89807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{k}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cfef805-d8bd-5a29-b7b3-ef894a92d11c" :answer/id #uuid "4cfef805-d8bd-5a29-b7b3-ef894a92d11c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf56fbd2-f5c2-579c-8ebb-6d499c95b621" :answer/id #uuid "bf56fbd2-f5c2-579c-8ebb-6d499c95b621" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{k}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fc0bc59-3785-5eda-b8fc-f947d9ab58ca" :answer/id #uuid "0fc0bc59-3785-5eda-b8fc-f947d9ab58ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{-2(k)^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8824de24-d3dc-5d98-b0ca-f43ee7699f9e" :answer/id #uuid "8824de24-d3dc-5d98-b0ca-f43ee7699f9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{k}^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13de8b24-aaa0-5ec2-ad2b-e4d7d0eec094" :question/id #uuid "13de8b24-aaa0-5ec2-ad2b-e4d7d0eec094" :question/math-academy-id "q-135712" :question/problem "Given that $q\\ge 0,$ simplify $12q-|-2q|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n12q-|2q|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12q-|2q| & = \\\\ 12q-|2|\\cdot |q| & = \\\\ 12q-2\\cdot |q| & = \\\\ 12q-2|q| & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $q\\ge 0,$ we have that $|q|=q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12q-2|q| & = \\\\ 12q-2q & = \\\\ 10q & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1a3138f-68f7-5a88-b85b-47ce925e2372"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1a3138f-68f7-5a88-b85b-47ce925e2372" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a3138f-68f7-5a88-b85b-47ce925e2372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44c2ade4-f5ae-5da7-92f6-13439bcfb0e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f20d18bf-7e28-55a2-8282-878eb89dcddf" "answer-c096d1ec-d8c4-558c-995c-07ceb55cc2fb" "answer-44c2ade4-f5ae-5da7-92f6-13439bcfb0e1" "answer-95da5035-aa03-5fd9-9934-1276912a83bc" "answer-b8b6c976-6a54-5ca3-9ff2-d2becacd1202"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f20d18bf-7e28-55a2-8282-878eb89dcddf" :answer/id #uuid "f20d18bf-7e28-55a2-8282-878eb89dcddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c096d1ec-d8c4-558c-995c-07ceb55cc2fb" :answer/id #uuid "c096d1ec-d8c4-558c-995c-07ceb55cc2fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44c2ade4-f5ae-5da7-92f6-13439bcfb0e1" :answer/id #uuid "44c2ade4-f5ae-5da7-92f6-13439bcfb0e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95da5035-aa03-5fd9-9934-1276912a83bc" :answer/id #uuid "95da5035-aa03-5fd9-9934-1276912a83bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8b6c976-6a54-5ca3-9ff2-d2becacd1202" :answer/id #uuid "b8b6c976-6a54-5ca3-9ff2-d2becacd1202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5e4373f-fc17-548d-be9f-bc3f6efcbaf7" :question/id #uuid "e5e4373f-fc17-548d-be9f-bc3f6efcbaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-135707" :question/problem "Given that $t\\ge 0,$ simplify $-36t+|-24t|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n-36t+|24t|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36t+|24t| & = \\\\ -36t+|24|\\cdot |t| & = \\\\ -36t+24\\cdot |t| & = \\\\ -36t+24|t| & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $t\\ge 0,$ we have that $|t|=t.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36t+24|t| & = \\\\ -36t+24t & = \\\\ -12t & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c92d9a2-3859-545b-a533-f16c27765483"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c92d9a2-3859-545b-a533-f16c27765483" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c92d9a2-3859-545b-a533-f16c27765483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78bde8d4-2f51-5499-a365-c45edac01872" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78bde8d4-2f51-5499-a365-c45edac01872" "answer-8e144f82-d6b5-5c17-89bc-8e3b36699d8d" "answer-fd72f5e5-6116-5149-af66-7c80c1a4d93d" "answer-aa08d3f1-d288-5d4f-bc40-67c27f035c8a" "answer-03e4c244-a68e-55ae-a92b-6540fc8c4b9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78bde8d4-2f51-5499-a365-c45edac01872" :answer/id #uuid "78bde8d4-2f51-5499-a365-c45edac01872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e144f82-d6b5-5c17-89bc-8e3b36699d8d" :answer/id #uuid "8e144f82-d6b5-5c17-89bc-8e3b36699d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-60t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd72f5e5-6116-5149-af66-7c80c1a4d93d" :answer/id #uuid "fd72f5e5-6116-5149-af66-7c80c1a4d93d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa08d3f1-d288-5d4f-bc40-67c27f035c8a" :answer/id #uuid "aa08d3f1-d288-5d4f-bc40-67c27f035c8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03e4c244-a68e-55ae-a92b-6540fc8c4b9c" :answer/id #uuid "03e4c244-a68e-55ae-a92b-6540fc8c4b9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173210 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of absolute value. \n\nLet's first remind ourselves of the rules of absolute value:\n\n- $|{-a}| = |a|$\n\n- $|a \\cdot b| = |a| \\cdot |b|$\n\n- $\\left| \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}$\n\nRecall that the last two rules are known as the **distributive properties.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173140 :tutorial/content "We know that the absolute value operation does nothing to a number if it is non-negative. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|0| & =0 \\\\ |1| & =1 \\\\ |2| & =2 \\\\ |3| & =3 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this fact as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| = x \\quad \\text{ if } \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe also know that $x^2$ is non-negative for any value of $x$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\left|x^2\\right| = x^2\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is *any* real number." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173141 :tutorial/content "We know that $|x|=x$ if $x \\geq 0.$ But what if $x < 0?$\n\nIf a variable is known to be negative, then the absolute value changes it to positive. To turn a negative number into a positive number, we multiply by $-1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n|x|  = -x \\quad\\textrm{for}\\quad x < 0.\n$$\n\nTo convince ourselves that this is indeed true, let's verify it in the case when $x=-2.$ Substituting this value into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-2| & =-(-2)=2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172208 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9c4f0f4c-398e-580b-8ef0-276b7b031bf0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9c4f0f4c-398e-580b-8ef0-276b7b031bf0" :question/id #uuid "9c4f0f4c-398e-580b-8ef0-276b7b031bf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133739" :question/problem "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Diana's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "The width of the plot is $x-1$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}-2x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},-2x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot (-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot (-1)) & = \\\\ 2x(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](images/da/dadb8090017f300275d803d488d401435b4f0e29b26ddf19e5542dba60a6bec4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd265226-df27-5582-ae30-81c64076603e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd265226-df27-5582-ae30-81c64076603e" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd265226-df27-5582-ae30-81c64076603e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0d8d48f2-1909-5037-a943-3e29c5504ed2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d8d48f2-1909-5037-a943-3e29c5504ed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d8d48f2-1909-5037-a943-3e29c5504ed2" :answer/id #uuid "0d8d48f2-1909-5037-a943-3e29c5504ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1194382f-adad-514e-8f5d-3f5219bb10e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1194382f-adad-514e-8f5d-3f5219bb10e2" :question/id #uuid "1194382f-adad-514e-8f5d-3f5219bb10e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133740" :question/problem "![](images/14/148968fddb7a50f2d021c366a63fdf42bacfe3f0c7cdbb7d844bc5cbeeeede9f.png)\n\nMike is Diana's neighbor. He has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-1$ square meters and whose width is $x-1$ meters, as depicted above. What is the length of Mike's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nMike's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the length of Mike's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1={x}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{1}^{2}=(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x+1$ meters.\n\n![](images/3c/3cb7db95170a088abd76aaf3f10a53ce50ff445a88d99349c76892d0ff4c8a05.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43240b2b-ba37-5fc0-b583-4caeb915e6d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43240b2b-ba37-5fc0-b583-4caeb915e6d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "43240b2b-ba37-5fc0-b583-4caeb915e6d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ac5bb48f-621d-5e4c-9556-d920b91e92ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac5bb48f-621d-5e4c-9556-d920b91e92ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac5bb48f-621d-5e4c-9556-d920b91e92ca" :answer/id #uuid "ac5bb48f-621d-5e4c-9556-d920b91e92ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054775 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1fe649a3-8b82-51f4-b2a6-c51dc888b658"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1fe649a3-8b82-51f4-b2a6-c51dc888b658" :question/id #uuid "1fe649a3-8b82-51f4-b2a6-c51dc888b658" :question/math-academy-id "q-133741" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e60f532665c5fdae2a9fdcebdc84af674cd9bdb8b1c820c7b766d565af74f1d1.png)\n\nA utility pole is located on the edge of Diana's land, as shown above. The distance from the right edge of Diana's plot to the pole is $x-3$ meters. Find the distance to the pole from the left edge as a function of $x.$\n\nThe required distance is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that the length of Diana's plot is $2x$ meters.\n\nTo find the required distance, we need to subtract polynomials $2x$ and $x-3:$\n\nWe put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)-(x-3) & = \\\\ 2x-x+3 & = \\\\ (2-1)x+3 & = \\\\ x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d65d922c-2c94-5032-93b1-bec03c4b0c50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d65d922c-2c94-5032-93b1-bec03c4b0c50" :answer-field/id #uuid "d65d922c-2c94-5032-93b1-bec03c4b0c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60880834-349f-5d68-8a44-911ad22f6c01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60880834-349f-5d68-8a44-911ad22f6c01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60880834-349f-5d68-8a44-911ad22f6c01" :answer/id #uuid "60880834-349f-5d68-8a44-911ad22f6c01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33fd3371-4ae9-5c23-8649-ebbfb1a6d062"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-33fd3371-4ae9-5c23-8649-ebbfb1a6d062" :question/id #uuid "33fd3371-4ae9-5c23-8649-ebbfb1a6d062" :question/math-academy-id "q-133742" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a0c1f1814427b696cff8ef4aaa7357744855a75598d5ed878dd93b022ed59de.png)\n\nDiana's neighbor to the south, Ron, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+x-6$ square meters, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Ron's plot of land is $x+3$ meters, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nRon's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+x-6.$ \n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-6).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-6) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 6 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-3) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-6) & =-5 & \\times  \\\\ -1+6 & =5 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-3) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ -2+3 & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $x=-2x+3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & ={x}^{2}-2x+3x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+3x-6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(3x-6) & = \\\\ x(x-2)+3(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ meters.  \n\n![](images/e6/e6330144c5ee2a25038e4378f442957a48df0e8dc06fa460f88863c4645fbecd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de5378f0-df33-5b0f-a0f2-946b1547ce91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de5378f0-df33-5b0f-a0f2-946b1547ce91" :answer-field/id #uuid "de5378f0-df33-5b0f-a0f2-946b1547ce91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2b3a0149-a358-598f-ab2f-8bf802871f13" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b3a0149-a358-598f-ab2f-8bf802871f13"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b3a0149-a358-598f-ab2f-8bf802871f13" :answer/id #uuid "2b3a0149-a358-598f-ab2f-8bf802871f13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078472 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a18950d-84c3-5e4c-9bb4-2650ee98898c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a18950d-84c3-5e4c-9bb4-2650ee98898c" :question/id #uuid "7a18950d-84c3-5e4c-9bb4-2650ee98898c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133743" :question/problem "What is the degree of the polynomial that represents the area of Ron's plot of land?\n\nDegree $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x-6\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{x}^{1}-6{x}^{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, it has the exponents $2,$ $1,$ and $0.$ The degree is the largest of these exponents, which is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-05dd90d2-4f2e-50be-840b-8eaf92978e29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-05dd90d2-4f2e-50be-840b-8eaf92978e29" :answer-field/id #uuid "05dd90d2-4f2e-50be-840b-8eaf92978e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d947ed4a-30ec-5732-81e7-778f31aa52e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d947ed4a-30ec-5732-81e7-778f31aa52e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d947ed4a-30ec-5732-81e7-778f31aa52e7" :answer/id #uuid "d947ed4a-30ec-5732-81e7-778f31aa52e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9c4d0824-bd55-5a10-a7aa-4a3af941bdbd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9c4d0824-bd55-5a10-a7aa-4a3af941bdbd" :question/id #uuid "9c4d0824-bd55-5a10-a7aa-4a3af941bdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-133744" :question/problem "If $x=12\\,\\text{m},$ what is the area of Ron's land plot?\n\nArea $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we have to evaluate the polynomial ${x}^{2}+x-6$ at $x=12.$\n\nSo, we substitute $x=12$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & = \\\\ (12)^{2}+(12)-6 & = \\\\ 144+12-6 & = \\\\ 150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is $150\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-594314f7-b3c5-5c92-a963-511c915f65b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-594314f7-b3c5-5c92-a963-511c915f65b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "594314f7-b3c5-5c92-a963-511c915f65b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-46756f20-d863-59c7-8831-681121f75c44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46756f20-d863-59c7-8831-681121f75c44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46756f20-d863-59c7-8831-681121f75c44" :answer/id #uuid "46756f20-d863-59c7-8831-681121f75c44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-324152f0-1462-5202-b328-1fba72a68453"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-324152f0-1462-5202-b328-1fba72a68453" :question/id #uuid "324152f0-1462-5202-b328-1fba72a68453" :question/math-academy-id "q-133745" :question/problem "![](images/18/183dd588b1fdb68cf3d428ca57f9d197379600e21d96be5974d86fcbe8155ca4.png)\n\nWhen $x=12\\,\\text{m},$ the dimensions of Ron's plot of land are $15\\,\\text{m}$ and $10\\text{m}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=15\\,\\text{m}$ and $b=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb349f11900012c4b129d93183ba511ab54f6b0e5e12cdc45e5579dd1d660dc1.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{325}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22b80e1f-d84c-5f29-a944-4327c56609dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22b80e1f-d84c-5f29-a944-4327c56609dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "22b80e1f-d84c-5f29-a944-4327c56609dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f283d800-81d1-5a56-b1e0-68088b147f00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f283d800-81d1-5a56-b1e0-68088b147f00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f283d800-81d1-5a56-b1e0-68088b147f00" :answer/id #uuid "f283d800-81d1-5a56-b1e0-68088b147f00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e11899b-f34e-5684-8a4e-df7b05889312"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e11899b-f34e-5684-8a4e-df7b05889312" :question/id #uuid "7e11899b-f34e-5684-8a4e-df7b05889312" :question/math-academy-id "q-133746" :question/problem "Diana purchased her land for $30$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Diana's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $30$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $30\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $30\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $30\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $30\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n30\\cdot (1.1)^{4}=43.923≈44,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $44$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0e31740-6395-5a0d-b9fa-1de55f602f35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0e31740-6395-5a0d-b9fa-1de55f602f35" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e31740-6395-5a0d-b9fa-1de55f602f35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-69854600-8512-5783-bdf7-d17d1483b014" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69854600-8512-5783-bdf7-d17d1483b014"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69854600-8512-5783-bdf7-d17d1483b014" :answer/id #uuid "69854600-8512-5783-bdf7-d17d1483b014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147563 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-feed4886-25a6-5bb9-8e0b-8564a97f415c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-feed4886-25a6-5bb9-8e0b-8564a97f415c" :question/id #uuid "feed4886-25a6-5bb9-8e0b-8564a97f415c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133747" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of Diana's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=30\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since she bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first three years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nDiana's land grew in value by approximately\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first three years." :question/worked-solution "To find the growth in the value over the first three years, we need to find the value after $3$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(3)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(3),$ the value of the land after $3$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =30\\cdot (1.1)^{(3)} \\\\  & =30\\cdot (1.331) \\\\  & =39.93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first three years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3)-V(0) & =39.93-30 \\\\  & =9.93 \\\\  & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first three years was approximately $10$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aca1f62c-80b7-588c-b643-a61820fa3d82"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aca1f62c-80b7-588c-b643-a61820fa3d82" :answer-field/id #uuid "aca1f62c-80b7-588c-b643-a61820fa3d82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b7415ceb-3b83-5136-9b39-6469c17c6deb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b7415ceb-3b83-5136-9b39-6469c17c6deb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7415ceb-3b83-5136-9b39-6469c17c6deb" :answer/id #uuid "b7415ceb-3b83-5136-9b39-6469c17c6deb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-99829926-a5fd-542b-80b4-e8fd9d5f741c" :multistep/id #uuid "99829926-a5fd-542b-80b4-e8fd9d5f741c" :multistep/context "![](images/d2/d231cfae7674f068409924ded4fa02835dd443d9ad008c2af82185d4f29b628c.png)\n\nDiana owns a rectangular plot of land, as shown above. The area of the plot is $2{x}^{2}-2x$ square meters, and its width is $x-1$ meters, where $x>6$ is a fixed constant." :multistep/steps ["step-72b50e48-8b06-5574-8aa6-6360a685a467" "step-f5acc739-822c-5f40-ac9e-a14d60de4098" "step-241ee0c1-0627-58cf-9746-9ede74b1fe3f" "step-9c9b7f8b-aac8-51c4-9595-0abb0b1ab465" "step-05672abe-ff66-5c08-b92b-f1a853192a0f" "step-f786a410-145a-582c-9cec-adc070d0f55e" "step-a1fbaa3e-1a9a-5fef-9082-119b8d093bfa" "step-bdf6f563-dcfb-5a5c-bf50-29ed05e49d98" "step-f6105d2e-2f55-59c9-8205-064386592867"] :multistep/first-step "step-72b50e48-8b06-5574-8aa6-6360a685a467" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-72b50e48-8b06-5574-8aa6-6360a685a467" :step/id #uuid "72b50e48-8b06-5574-8aa6-6360a685a467" :step/content "question-9c4f0f4c-398e-580b-8ef0-276b7b031bf0" :step/next "step-f5acc739-822c-5f40-ac9e-a14d60de4098" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f5acc739-822c-5f40-ac9e-a14d60de4098" :step/id #uuid "f5acc739-822c-5f40-ac9e-a14d60de4098" :step/content "question-1194382f-adad-514e-8f5d-3f5219bb10e2" :step/next "step-241ee0c1-0627-58cf-9746-9ede74b1fe3f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-241ee0c1-0627-58cf-9746-9ede74b1fe3f" :step/id #uuid "241ee0c1-0627-58cf-9746-9ede74b1fe3f" :step/content "question-1fe649a3-8b82-51f4-b2a6-c51dc888b658" :step/next "step-9c9b7f8b-aac8-51c4-9595-0abb0b1ab465" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9c9b7f8b-aac8-51c4-9595-0abb0b1ab465" :step/id #uuid "9c9b7f8b-aac8-51c4-9595-0abb0b1ab465" :step/content "question-33fd3371-4ae9-5c23-8649-ebbfb1a6d062" :step/next "step-05672abe-ff66-5c08-b92b-f1a853192a0f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-05672abe-ff66-5c08-b92b-f1a853192a0f" :step/id #uuid "05672abe-ff66-5c08-b92b-f1a853192a0f" :step/content "question-7a18950d-84c3-5e4c-9bb4-2650ee98898c" :step/next "step-f786a410-145a-582c-9cec-adc070d0f55e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f786a410-145a-582c-9cec-adc070d0f55e" :step/id #uuid "f786a410-145a-582c-9cec-adc070d0f55e" :step/content "question-9c4d0824-bd55-5a10-a7aa-4a3af941bdbd" :step/next "step-a1fbaa3e-1a9a-5fef-9082-119b8d093bfa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a1fbaa3e-1a9a-5fef-9082-119b8d093bfa" :step/id #uuid "a1fbaa3e-1a9a-5fef-9082-119b8d093bfa" :step/content "question-324152f0-1462-5202-b328-1fba72a68453" :step/next "step-bdf6f563-dcfb-5a5c-bf50-29ed05e49d98" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bdf6f563-dcfb-5a5c-bf50-29ed05e49d98" :step/id #uuid "bdf6f563-dcfb-5a5c-bf50-29ed05e49d98" :step/content "question-7e11899b-f34e-5684-8a4e-df7b05889312" :step/next "step-f6105d2e-2f55-59c9-8205-064386592867" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f6105d2e-2f55-59c9-8205-064386592867" :step/id #uuid "f6105d2e-2f55-59c9-8205-064386592867" :step/content "question-feed4886-25a6-5bb9-8e0b-8564a97f415c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-41cfe6f0-7c05-51b8-ae43-301b2d263858" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "41cfe6f0-7c05-51b8-ae43-301b2d263858" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-99829926-a5fd-542b-80b4-e8fd9d5f741c" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186051017 17592186048928 17592186048524 17592186049074 17592186048406 17592186050396 17592186049224 17592186050886 17592186048650] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-43a9386c-2730-5895-97fc-f584b3b78e2c" :activity/id #uuid "43a9386c-2730-5895-97fc-f584b3b78e2c" :activity/title "Factoring Polynomials Using Various Techniques" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-91995cd2-dc8f-5cda-b179-04dffce555d6"] :activity/first-step "step-91995cd2-dc8f-5cda-b179-04dffce555d6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-91995cd2-dc8f-5cda-b179-04dffce555d6" :step/id #uuid "91995cd2-dc8f-5cda-b179-04dffce555d6" :step/content "assigned-problem-41cfe6f0-7c05-51b8-ae43-301b2d263858" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186056658 :answer-field/choices 17592186056659] [:db/retract 17592186056658 :answer-field/choices 17592186056660] [:db/retract 17592186056658 :answer-field/choices 17592186056661] [:db/retract 17592186056658 :answer-field/choices 17592186056662] [:db/retract 17592186056658 :answer-field/choices 17592186056663] [:db/retract 17592186056682 :answer-field/choices 17592186056683] [:db/retract 17592186056682 :answer-field/choices 17592186056684] [:db/retract 17592186056682 :answer-field/choices 17592186056685] [:db/retract 17592186056682 :answer-field/choices 17592186056686] [:db/retract 17592186056682 :answer-field/choices 17592186056687] {:db/id 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" "question-b68ac86a-b851-54a2-a0bc-3666201fc505" "question-1c22a90f-9b7c-5ddf-a251-f5b0ff5c7b6d" "question-8fa7b8f1-fff9-5728-a4e4-0be7942c5cc6" "question-3c88652f-90de-5af1-9dba-66fecdb99433"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059384 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=-5,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ -5\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-87f55a60-f84e-5cb1-9665-2c11dc9713b6" "question-4c371072-128a-5610-adfb-0397399d02d6" "question-cba5f850-77ea-5dbe-8551-4310d69ae456" "question-64f21f0a-233c-5960-8d23-c7d29d8f3ed3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059382 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=196$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=24,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 196 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{196}} \\\\ 24\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66172e40-7344-58fd-a3d5-83fcbd16bd0f" "question-841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" "question-40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" "question-bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059383 :question/problem "The daily percentage return of a particular stock has a population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5\\%.$ A sample of $90$ randomly selected trading days was examined, and it was found that the mean daily return of the stock over these days was $7.5\\%.$ Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean daily percentage return $\\mu$ of the entire population of trading days.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 90\\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=7.5, \\qquad\nn = 90, \\qquad\n\\sigma = 5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.05}=1.645{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{90}} \\\\ 7.5\\pm 0.867\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" :question/id #uuid "2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142973" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=12$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=9,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\ 9\\pm 1.899\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f02c5f87-b3cc-5c21-82fd-c00777a3a5e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f02c5f87-b3cc-5c21-82fd-c00777a3a5e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f02c5f87-b3cc-5c21-82fd-c00777a3a5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8299b9d2-aeff-5a18-a5df-fba1e994818a" "answer-d3cba11e-5f53-5e85-aa22-88f50ef82d60" "answer-b0947152-9db1-51dc-96a4-f6cccad66ada" "answer-58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" "answer-668c3880-6cf7-5f86-8d9d-c48f288c2489"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "a