ere $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 22\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f832b1d1-a2f7-5a16-9ffd-1dcb274b8fae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f832b1d1-a2f7-5a16-9ffd-1dcb274b8fae" :answer-field/id #uuid "f832b1d1-a2f7-5a16-9ffd-1dcb274b8fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2bc03372-7346-5af4-aa8f-947357c87bc6" "answer-18db88ac-9193-5677-bce3-d86fd6469a6e" "answer-01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" "answer-355a5122-c31e-5d74-bdf3-1044eb577532" "answer-fa43971d-f124-5992-a95a-4b9b70ecf9b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bc03372-7346-5af4-aa8f-947357c87bc6" :answer/id #uuid "2bc03372-7346-5af4-aa8f-947357c87bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pm 1.123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18db88ac-9193-5677-bce3-d86fd6469a6e" :answer/id #uuid "18db88ac-9193-5677-bce3-d86fd6469a6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19\\pm 1.156" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer/id #uuid "01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\pm 0.823" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-355a5122-c31e-5d74-bdf3-1044eb577532" :answer/id #uuid "355a5122-c31e-5d74-bdf3-1044eb577532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\pm 0.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa43971d-f124-5992-a95a-4b9b70ecf9b2" :answer/id #uuid "fa43971d-f124-5992-a95a-4b9b70ecf9b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\pm 1.252" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060050 :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =5.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=14,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 14\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056658 :answer-field/correct "answer-0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" "answer-a11f5764-4f74-5c87-95e2-5a3a414d074a" "answer-7691218c-ed40-5f8c-9d71-ed78baa0649c" "answer-67d46b32-db24-5401-952b-f0869939e48c" "answer-29796b67-7bcc-55f0-9517-3abd2cef4b6b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" :answer/id #uuid "0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pm 0.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a11f5764-4f74-5c87-95e2-5a3a414d074a" :answer/id #uuid "a11f5764-4f74-5c87-95e2-5a3a414d074a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pm 1.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7691218c-ed40-5f8c-9d71-ed78baa0649c" :answer/id #uuid "7691218c-ed40-5f8c-9d71-ed78baa0649c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pm 2.56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67d46b32-db24-5401-952b-f0869939e48c" :answer/id #uuid "67d46b32-db24-5401-952b-f0869939e48c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\pm 0.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29796b67-7bcc-55f0-9517-3abd2cef4b6b" :answer/id #uuid "29796b67-7bcc-55f0-9517-3abd2cef4b6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\pm 1.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-87f55a60-f84e-5cb1-9665-2c11dc9713b6" :question/id #uuid "87f55a60-f84e-5cb1-9665-2c11dc9713b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142500" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=144$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=144\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{144}} \\\\ 10\\pm 0.429\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fab1b25-a070-5368-a780-857e62a9b5a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fab1b25-a070-5368-a780-857e62a9b5a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fab1b25-a070-5368-a780-857e62a9b5a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16f87830-e8d8-51ae-82fa-53af8da47616" "answer-6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" "answer-4a605826-710d-5524-b97d-c06b411fd2fd" "answer-5fecd59c-92b6-5dd8-9e61-56150279d05d" "answer-00d5769c-b5ff-5fe6-bfd4-f75f9a168407"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f87830-e8d8-51ae-82fa-53af8da47616" :answer/id #uuid "16f87830-e8d8-51ae-82fa-53af8da47616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pm 0.425" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" :answer/id #uuid "6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pm 0.429" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a605826-710d-5524-b97d-c06b411fd2fd" :answer/id #uuid "4a605826-710d-5524-b97d-c06b411fd2fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pm 0.713" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fecd59c-92b6-5dd8-9e61-56150279d05d" :answer/id #uuid "5fecd59c-92b6-5dd8-9e61-56150279d05d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 0.718" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00d5769c-b5ff-5fe6-bfd4-f75f9a168407" :answer/id #uuid "00d5769c-b5ff-5fe6-bfd4-f75f9a168407" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14.4\\pm 2.576" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c371072-128a-5610-adfb-0397399d02d6" :question/id #uuid "4c371072-128a-5610-adfb-0397399d02d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142491" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=80\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.438\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-013e8c02-58d5-5e41-b09e-a87d19761e14"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-013e8c02-58d5-5e41-b09e-a87d19761e14" :answer-field/id #uuid "013e8c02-58d5-5e41-b09e-a87d19761e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" "answer-5245fbae-682c-5200-a3cc-b240ba6e18a1" "answer-8ac017e5-f420-5574-9450-e76853f09d22" "answer-bfe37f3a-324e-5cb5-84ab-a56302e24787" "answer-9f618acf-fb41-51a6-99f4-2df360eb2078"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/id #uuid "3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.438" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5245fbae-682c-5200-a3cc-b240ba6e18a1" :answer/id #uuid "5245fbae-682c-5200-a3cc-b240ba6e18a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.490" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ac017e5-f420-5574-9450-e76853f09d22" :answer/id #uuid "8ac017e5-f420-5574-9450-e76853f09d22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.392" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfe37f3a-324e-5cb5-84ab-a56302e24787" :answer/id #uuid "bfe37f3a-324e-5cb5-84ab-a56302e24787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 0.859" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f618acf-fb41-51a6-99f4-2df360eb2078" :answer/id #uuid "9f618acf-fb41-51a6-99f4-2df360eb2078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\pm 4.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cba5f850-77ea-5dbe-8551-4310d69ae456" :question/id #uuid "cba5f850-77ea-5dbe-8551-4310d69ae456" :question/math-academy-id "q-142503" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=225$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-12,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=225\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ -12\\pm 0.687\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07edf53b-4692-5fb0-bd69-4d9293ef1c71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07edf53b-4692-5fb0-bd69-4d9293ef1c71" :answer-field/id #uuid "07edf53b-4692-5fb0-bd69-4d9293ef1c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3afa5f38-b925-516d-80ee-5acdd056b9f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d18e529-adf9-5b89-94e9-0fde27f40cb6" "answer-3afa5f38-b925-516d-80ee-5acdd056b9f7" "answer-d7967b2c-1a0b-5e85-9c4a-b0d62d8d2406" "answer-6a724e2c-cb0b-5c9c-adcf-1e12eb478a7e" "answer-0c7c535d-0d9a-5a44-a538-4f046c0f3916"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d18e529-adf9-5b89-94e9-0fde27f40cb6" :answer/id #uuid "3d18e529-adf9-5b89-94e9-0fde27f40cb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.183" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3afa5f38-b925-516d-80ee-5acdd056b9f7" :answer/id #uuid "3afa5f38-b925-516d-80ee-5acdd056b9f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.687" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7967b2c-1a0b-5e85-9c4a-b0d62d8d2406" :answer/id #uuid "d7967b2c-1a0b-5e85-9c4a-b0d62d8d2406" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.831" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a724e2c-cb0b-5c9c-adcf-1e12eb478a7e" :answer/id #uuid "6a724e2c-cb0b-5c9c-adcf-1e12eb478a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.867" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c7c535d-0d9a-5a44-a538-4f046c0f3916" :answer/id #uuid "0c7c535d-0d9a-5a44-a538-4f046c0f3916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pm 1.748" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64f21f0a-233c-5960-8d23-c7d29d8f3ed3" :question/id #uuid "64f21f0a-233c-5960-8d23-c7d29d8f3ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142497" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=135$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =1.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=135\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{135}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.142\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77f21f06-2475-57bd-a2cb-3044a83d5e18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77f21f06-2475-57bd-a2cb-3044a83d5e18" :answer-field/id #uuid "77f21f06-2475-57bd-a2cb-3044a83d5e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer-field/choices ["answer-74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" "answer-26f43a97-858d-5d6b-8e14-2ca1af1707b5" "answer-4d69db6f-7ccc-5702-a556-c06d016e9316" "answer-419aefd4-69a2-580b-b986-0da3f154ef5a" "answer-4e998fe4-61fb-520e-8733-db8847569f9f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/id #uuid "74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.142" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26f43a97-858d-5d6b-8e14-2ca1af1707b5" :answer/id #uuid "26f43a97-858d-5d6b-8e14-2ca1af1707b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d69db6f-7ccc-5702-a556-c06d016e9316" :answer/id #uuid "4d69db6f-7ccc-5702-a556-c06d016e9316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 1.645" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419aefd4-69a2-580b-b986-0da3f154ef5a" :answer/id #uuid "419aefd4-69a2-580b-b986-0da3f154ef5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e998fe4-61fb-520e-8733-db8847569f9f" :answer/id #uuid "4e998fe4-61fb-520e-8733-db8847569f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135\\pm 3.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-66172e40-7344-58fd-a3d5-83fcbd16bd0f" :question/id #uuid "66172e40-7344-58fd-a3d5-83fcbd16bd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143021" :question/problem "The daily income of a supermarket is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =1\\,200$ dollars. During a particular $28$-working-day month, the mean daily income was $1\\,950$ dollars. Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean daily income $\\mu $ of every work day.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=1\\,950,\\,n=28,\\,\\sigma =1\\,200\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,950\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1\\,200}{\\sqrt{28}} \\\\ 1\\,950\\pm 373\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32c160c3-a273-5a41-b70a-aa88ce3a1e92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32c160c3-a273-5a41-b70a-aa88ce3a1e92" :answer-field/id #uuid "32c160c3-a273-5a41-b70a-aa88ce3a1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" "answer-ad0fb17a-427f-52ae-a47b-05969843f0f5" "answer-2ef1c593-b4c7-5222-b654-ac60ee16ce57" "answer-a31c3860-8bc1-5373-9bd3-cf961c516f73" "answer-4dc705e2-76e5-5216-86a9-dd85770b73a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" :answer/id #uuid "9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 373$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad0fb17a-427f-52ae-a47b-05969843f0f5" :answer/id #uuid "ad0fb17a-427f-52ae-a47b-05969843f0f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 415$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ef1c593-b4c7-5222-b654-ac60ee16ce57" :answer/id #uuid "2ef1c593-b4c7-5222-b654-ac60ee16ce57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 528$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a31c3860-8bc1-5373-9bd3-cf961c516f73" :answer/id #uuid "a31c3860-8bc1-5373-9bd3-cf961c516f73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 584$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dc705e2-76e5-5216-86a9-dd85770b73a9" :answer/id #uuid "4dc705e2-76e5-5216-86a9-dd85770b73a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 71$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" :question/id #uuid "841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-143022" :question/problem "In order to measure the acceleration due to gravity, Annie knocks a ball off a table and records the time it takes to reach the ground. The measurements of the falling time of the ball are normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.2\\,s.$ She repeats the experiment $16$ times and obtains a mean falling time of $0.55\\,s.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean falling time $\\mu .$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=0.55\\,s,\\,n=16,\\,\\sigma =0.2\\,s\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.55\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 0.55\\pm 0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74b47122-e0ef-5f16-b996-8efddca3bab2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74b47122-e0ef-5f16-b996-8efddca3bab2" :answer-field/id #uuid "74b47122-e0ef-5f16-b996-8efddca3bab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbfcdb9f-6c30-5b43-b4de-181ea4940af1" "answer-8e3795ed-bdaf-5738-93ed-2a1185daa06c" "answer-327a0039-b06e-53dd-bfc8-383328623244" "answer-cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" "answer-155ad1bd-d997-50f4-ab96-9007afb194f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbfcdb9f-6c30-5b43-b4de-181ea4940af1" :answer/id #uuid "cbfcdb9f-6c30-5b43-b4de-181ea4940af1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3795ed-bdaf-5738-93ed-2a1185daa06c" :answer/id #uuid "8e3795ed-bdaf-5738-93ed-2a1185daa06c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-327a0039-b06e-53dd-bfc8-383328623244" :answer/id #uuid "327a0039-b06e-53dd-bfc8-383328623244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/id #uuid "cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-155ad1bd-d997-50f4-ab96-9007afb194f1" :answer/id #uuid "155ad1bd-d997-50f4-ab96-9007afb194f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75\\pm 0.13\\,s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060054 :question/problem "The weight of apples in a particular harvest is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.$ A farmer sampled $10$ apples from the harvest and found that their mean weight is $100\\,\\text{g}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire harvest. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=100\\,\\text{g},\\,n=10,\\,\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 100\\pm 9.775\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056682 :answer-field/correct "answer-f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5523cdb-710f-5180-88b7-c913d39f57ce" "answer-03590c25-5754-5f22-a19f-227f931d9ca2" "answer-f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" "answer-31773c72-42fa-5a91-a142-e52eda53327f" "answer-336113ef-d7ab-5192-b8d5-39d93c496600"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5523cdb-710f-5180-88b7-c913d39f57ce" :answer/id #uuid "b5523cdb-710f-5180-88b7-c913d39f57ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\pm 12.271\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03590c25-5754-5f22-a19f-227f931d9ca2" :answer/id #uuid "03590c25-5754-5f22-a19f-227f931d9ca2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" :answer/id #uuid "f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\pm 9.775\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31773c72-42fa-5a91-a142-e52eda53327f" :answer/id #uuid "31773c72-42fa-5a91-a142-e52eda53327f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-336113ef-d7ab-5192-b8d5-39d93c496600" :answer/id #uuid "336113ef-d7ab-5192-b8d5-39d93c496600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pm 9.775\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" :question/id #uuid "40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-143080" :question/problem "The length, in centimeters, of newborn children in a particular hospital is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =5\\,cm.$ On a particular day in a nursery room of the hospital, there are $64$ newborn children, and their mean length is $49.5\\,cm.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean length $\\mu $ of the children born in that hospital.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=49.5\\,cm,\\,n=64,\\,\\sigma =5\\,cm.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49.5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 49.5\\pm 1.61\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bd35d8c-117b-596a-9cbd-63108e62ec80"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bd35d8c-117b-596a-9cbd-63108e62ec80" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd35d8c-117b-596a-9cbd-63108e62ec80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4593f573-da01-5e58-91b2-b20a7f84bb52" "answer-75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" "answer-fc5b7218-6c68-5e18-b2b9-35212faa3729" "answer-5b2069e1-e324-5627-8cfa-51a17d78e4d0" "answer-44c8f4e3-9a6e-50ab-a851-2ca0685d61a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4593f573-da01-5e58-91b2-b20a7f84bb52" :answer/id #uuid "4593f573-da01-5e58-91b2-b20a7f84bb52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.22\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/id #uuid "75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.61\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc5b7218-6c68-5e18-b2b9-35212faa3729" :answer/id #uuid "fc5b7218-6c68-5e18-b2b9-35212faa3729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.64\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b2069e1-e324-5627-8cfa-51a17d78e4d0" :answer/id #uuid "5b2069e1-e324-5627-8cfa-51a17d78e4d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.5\\pm 3.14\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44c8f4e3-9a6e-50ab-a851-2ca0685d61a8" :answer/id #uuid "44c8f4e3-9a6e-50ab-a851-2ca0685d61a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.5\\pm 4.12\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6" :question/id #uuid "bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143123" :question/problem "The amount of wine, in hectoliters, produced by one hectare of a vineyard in a particular region is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,hl.$ A sample of $120$ hectares is examined, and it is found that the mean volume of wine produced by each hectare is $15\\,hl.$\nFind a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean hectoliters per hectare $\\mu $ of the entire region.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,hl,\\,n=120,\\,\\sigma =2\\,hl.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 15\\pm 0.470\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02a05532-9f78-5d65-a4d0-0f27f0b07a9f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02a05532-9f78-5d65-a4d0-0f27f0b07a9f" :answer-field/id #uuid "02a05532-9f78-5d65-a4d0-0f27f0b07a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45c91c8a-9fcd-528c-80ed-69a7d1d14cc0" "answer-a6df01ca-23cd-5023-bc1b-30fd13a5143d" "answer-0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" "answer-64fc6af7-ef67-5b4b-80fc-566d1be773ca" "answer-15cb3f12-38ac-5ae3-b103-cecbd2328a68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c91c8a-9fcd-528c-80ed-69a7d1d14cc0" :answer/id #uuid "45c91c8a-9fcd-528c-80ed-69a7d1d14cc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pm 0.008\\,hl" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6df01ca-23cd-5023-bc1b-30fd13a5143d" :answer/id #uuid "a6df01ca-23cd-5023-bc1b-30fd13a5143d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pm 0.358\\,hl" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/id #uuid "0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pm 0.470\\,hl" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64fc6af7-ef67-5b4b-80fc-566d1be773ca" :answer/id #uuid "64fc6af7-ef67-5b4b-80fc-566d1be773ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pm 2.684\\,hl" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15cb3f12-38ac-5ae3-b103-cecbd2328a68" :answer/id #uuid "15cb3f12-38ac-5ae3-b103-cecbd2328a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pm 3.527\\,hl" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060964 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=10$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2=9.$ After processing our results, we compute the mean of this sample using the usual methods and get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=12\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is unsatisfactory because we have no information regarding its reliability.\n\nFor this reason, it's often more helpful to give a range of possible values for $\\mu.$ In other words, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x},$ we might instead report a so-called **confidence interval,** which is an interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is our estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is some **margin of error**.\n\nIn addition, we need to give some indication regarding the reliability of our confidence interval. With this in mind, we will construct a $\\boldsymbol{95\\%}$ **confidence interval** for the population mean $\\mu$. The precise meaning of this will become clear shortly." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060968 :tutorial/content "In our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.96< & \\,Z<1.96 \\\\ -1.96< & \\,\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}<1.96 \\\\ -1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,-\\mu \\,<-\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}> & \\,\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\\: \\overline{x} + 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=10, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=12, \\qquad\n\\sigma=3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 12- 1.859, \\: 12 + 1.859 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060967 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/58/581fc2da88e4419e78389a1b5eedb127f590641d0b7ee21c489ed85184795b13.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060966 :tutorial/content "It's important to be able to interpret confidence intervals correctly.\n\nImagine we conduct several independent random samples from a population. The mean of the sample will be different for each sample. Some values of $\\overline{x}$ might lie close to the population mean $\\mu,$ and some could lie further away.\n\nSuppose we construct a $90\\%$ confidence interval for each sample and plot them on the same diagram. We might get a picture similar to the one below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc8dd707751cd9e1cb0da1bf618b6d7aed740fab875d8d49c7c89fc09b77321e.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, we see that nine out of ten of our confidence intervals contain $\\mu,$ while one of them (the red one) does *not* contain the population mean $\\mu.$ \n\nThe idea is that if we construct many $90\\%$ confidence intervals, one for each sample, then approximately $90\\%$ of these confidence intervals will contain the population mean $\\mu.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060965 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use our formula for the confidence interval of the population mean $\\mu{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059935 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186173974 :answer-field/choices 17592186173975] [:db/retract 17592186173974 :answer-field/choices 17592186173976] [:db/retract 17592186173974 :answer-field/choices 17592186173977] [:db/retract 17592186173974 :answer-field/choices 17592186173978] [:db/retract 17592186173974 :answer-field/choices 17592186173979] {:db/id 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6053da2-f1d5-5511-b304-aa8032f24e40" "question-4b666acc-0963-5776-a5f2-9fbed9a3deef" "question-36c5f89e-4486-5d52-bdc6-04e04734d5f5" "question-c3a863b6-3f9a-5678-9da7-4af2ad4a2999" "question-a2d92ca9-1354-51b9-b571-e879ca411a35"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175865 :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(-1,2^2), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim  N(3, 3^2), \\qquad \nX_3\\sim N(4,2)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y = X_1 + X_2 + X_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1),\\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2),\\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(-1,2^2), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim  N(3, 3^2), \\qquad \nX_3\\sim N(4,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = X_1 + X_2 + X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-1+3+4 \\\\  & =6, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =4+9+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(6,15).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176038 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c34156c-74ee-5271-a0cf-be78f0930f45" "question-f0eb5f1d-3690-5932-81c7-b92330f2d368" "question-0dfaf0ac-f717-5f63-800c-8310dd062b12" "question-b4dc7b39-09f7-500e-8d29-5718ad3819f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175685 :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(-1,3), \\qquad \nX_2 \\sim  N(3,2), \\qquad \nX_3 \\sim N(2,1)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y = X_1 - 2X_2 - X_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1), \\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nand $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(-1,3), \\qquad \nX_2 \\sim  N(3,2), \\qquad \nX_3 \\sim N(2,1),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = X_1 - 2X_2 - X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-6-2 \\\\  & =-9, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3+8+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3} & ∼N(-9,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176013 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d3c786de-a1dd-576c-8250-cdb70d678da3" "question-fef171e3-d5a0-5ed0-9b02-411a8ba77086" "question-29e90455-0d48-5325-bbcb-97a6b476016a" "question-1ed551ba-20e8-589e-8222-2bcdb2f9bd69" "question-7f3eef4b-44d7-5d7f-9ad0-9c61a5d17d35"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175866 :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(4,5), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim N(-2,2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(3,1)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y=3X_1-3X_2-X_3,$ calculate $P(|Y| \\lt 3).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.04 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.05 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0129 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0125 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0122 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0166 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0162 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0158 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.0 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0212 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0207 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0202 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.5 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0668 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0655 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0643 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.4 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0808 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0793 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0778 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.3 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0968 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0951 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0934 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1), \\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nand $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(4,5), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim N(-2,2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(3,1),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=3X_1-3X_2-X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5+(-3)^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5+9\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(15,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y |\\lt  3)=P(-3 <  Y \\lt  3).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<Y<3) & =P(\\frac{-3-15}{\\sqrt{64}}<Z<\\frac{3-15}{\\sqrt{64}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.25<Z<-1.5) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.5)-P(Z<-2.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.5)-Φ(-2.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.5)≈0.0668,\\,Φ(-2.25)≈0.0122.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|<3) & =Φ(-1.5)-Φ(-2.25) \\\\  & ≈0.0668-0.0122 \\\\  & =0.0546.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176039 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-64b4d9dd-0ca6-5664-aa15-145818c765b0" "question-dd5e602a-e46f-521b-9487-8ddb9d6bb489" "question-78e73be3-8be0-5432-b894-38767fce09cc" "question-4a63a0a4-bc99-5394-8795-605d4e119f34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175686 :question/problem "A shop sells $2$ types of rice, Type $A$ and Type $B.$ The volume per packet for Type $A$ rice is normally distributed with a mean of $1$ liter and a variance of $0.01$ liters2, while the volume per packet for Type $B$ rice is normally distributed with a mean of $0.7$ liters and a variance of $0.02$ liters2. During cooking, it is known that Type $A$ rice quadruples in volume, while Type $B$ rice triples in volume.\n\nIf a single package of Type $A$ rice and $2$ packages of Type $B$ rice are randomly selected and cooked, what is the probability that the resulting rice volume is less than $8$ liters? You may assume that the volumes of rice in the packages are mutually independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3594 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3974 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4364 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4325 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_1$ denote the volume of the Type $A$ rice package, and let $X_2$ and $X_3$ denote the volumes of the Type $B$ rice packages. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(1, 0.01), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(0.7, 0.02), \\qquad X_3\\sim N(0.7, 0.02).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total resulting volume of rice after cooking. Since the Type $A$ rice quadruples in volume, while the type $B$ rice triples in volume, we have\n\n$$\nY= 4X_1+3X_2 + 3X_3.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0.7+3\\cdot 0.7) \\\\  & =8.2 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 0.01+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.02+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0.01+9\\cdot 0.02+9\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(8.2, 0.52).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y \\lt 8),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<8) & =P(Z<\\frac{8-8.2}{\\sqrt{0.52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.28) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.28)\\approx 0.3897.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<8) & ≈Φ(-0.28) \\\\  & ≈0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6053da2-f1d5-5511-b304-aa8032f24e40" :question/id #uuid "d6053da2-f1d5-5511-b304-aa8032f24e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-142941" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,{3}^{2})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+0 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+2+9 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(4,15).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-277c902d-e34f-5883-b47e-dce978610a9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-277c902d-e34f-5883-b47e-dce978610a9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "277c902d-e34f-5883-b47e-dce978610a9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-474c014b-6c51-580e-8f35-4173a8bdc5af" "answer-957ed374-2949-53b9-a263-375ab1c354f9" "answer-cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" "answer-75ccee7a-ee30-58cb-9659-bc3ed84a9f1d" "answer-af78e032-61cc-5c78-97ac-8a372331d0fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-474c014b-6c51-580e-8f35-4173a8bdc5af" :answer/id #uuid "474c014b-6c51-580e-8f35-4173a8bdc5af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(2,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-957ed374-2949-53b9-a263-375ab1c354f9" :answer/id #uuid "957ed374-2949-53b9-a263-375ab1c354f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(3,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" :answer/id #uuid "cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(4,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75ccee7a-ee30-58cb-9659-bc3ed84a9f1d" :answer/id #uuid "75ccee7a-ee30-58cb-9659-bc3ed84a9f1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(4,49)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af78e032-61cc-5c78-97ac-8a372331d0fb" :answer/id #uuid "af78e032-61cc-5c78-97ac-8a372331d0fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(4,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b666acc-0963-5776-a5f2-9fbed9a3deef" :question/id #uuid "4b666acc-0963-5776-a5f2-9fbed9a3deef" :question/math-academy-id "q-142935" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-7,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,3)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-7,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,3),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3+(-7)+5 \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(1,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6272a243-0d5a-50d0-8046-f6e45f838f2c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6272a243-0d5a-50d0-8046-f6e45f838f2c" :answer-field/id #uuid "6272a243-0d5a-50d0-8046-f6e45f838f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52512766-78b5-5354-a8d5-e0d1d50160cb" "answer-e7d11e4f-b65d-5603-aa21-940c487b15e5" "answer-1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" "answer-e43a496c-422e-5091-8a74-3dfa5e0aaf71" "answer-b4bd4fc9-def5-513d-9ca7-7f8f3f8a2d37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52512766-78b5-5354-a8d5-e0d1d50160cb" :answer/id #uuid "52512766-78b5-5354-a8d5-e0d1d50160cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,14)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7d11e4f-b65d-5603-aa21-940c487b15e5" :answer/id #uuid "e7d11e4f-b65d-5603-aa21-940c487b15e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,36)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" :answer/id #uuid "1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e43a496c-422e-5091-8a74-3dfa5e0aaf71" :answer/id #uuid "e43a496c-422e-5091-8a74-3dfa5e0aaf71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(15,14)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4bd4fc9-def5-513d-9ca7-7f8f3f8a2d37" :answer/id #uuid "b4bd4fc9-def5-513d-9ca7-7f8f3f8a2d37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(15,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-36c5f89e-4486-5d52-bdc6-04e04734d5f5" :question/id #uuid "36c5f89e-4486-5d52-bdc6-04e04734d5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142946" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,{3}^{2})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-3+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-3, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+{5}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+9 \\\\  & =38.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(-3,38).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-781fa7f1-3abb-5d7b-8415-6a4dfad64c9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-781fa7